554:
(a) Roadways may be created by:
(1) Dedication;
(2) Prescription;
(3) Express grant of an easement;
(4) Deed;
(5) Special statutory proceeding; or
(6) Condemnation.
(b) The board of commissioners has determined that there shall be the following classification of roadways in the county:
(1) Private roadways. A private road is a roadway that is created by an easement from a land owner to a certain individual or individuals and the use of said road can be controlled by the parties to the easement. It has been determined by the county zoning ordinance that there can be only one building permit issued by a private easement roadway.
(2) Public roadways. A public roadway is a roadway where the general public has acquired an implied easement for a roadway over the property of others by virtue of the unobstructed use of said roadway for a sufficient length of time to establish a public easement over the roadway. Public roadways are roadways over which the public has the right to travel, but have not been accepted by the governing authority of the county as county roads and the county assumes no responsibility to maintain said roadways.
Upon compliance with all of the provisions of the county zoning ordinance, building permits will be issued for building on public roads upon the applicant signing a "statement of understanding" that said roadway is a public road and that the county assumes no responsibility for the maintenance of said road until such time as it becomes feasible and advisable to accept said roadway as a "county road." The county at such time will accept said road as a county road upon the property owners along said road conveying a right-of-way to the county conveying the minimum width required by the county.
(3) County roadways. A county roadway is one that has been acquired by dedication, by prescription, by the express grant of an easement, by deed, by statutory proceedings, or by condemnation, and further has been accepted by the governing authority of the county and designated as a "county road" and has assumed the responsibility to maintain said road for the general public. Upon compliance with the provisions of the county zoning ordinance, building permits will be issued on county roads.
(Res. No. 82-36, §§ I—IV, 11-16-82)

555:
(a) Findings of fact; scope:
(1) Henry County encompasses an area of three hundred thirty-one (331) square miles and at the present time has approximately three hundred sixteen (316) miles of unpaved roads and approximately three hundred seventy (370) miles of paved roads;
(2) The county is in the process of completing a road inventory showing all of the roads in Henry County and the classification of said roads as they exist;
(3) The governing authority of Henry County has determined that there are certain unpaved roads within the county that are not used for the general public benefit and, it is not economically feasible or legally justifiable for the taxpayers of the county to maintain these roadways, some of which are no more than private driveways, and these roadways, if they have been maintained by the county, will be abandoned as public roads in accordance with the Official Code of Georgia, Sections 32-7-1 and 32-7-2; and
(4) It is the desire of the governing authority of Henry County to adopt the following policy concerning the types, uses and maintenance schedule of the unpaved roads in the county.
(b) Creation or establishment of roadways. Roadways must be created by:
(1) Dedication;
(2) Prescription;
(3) Express grant of an easement;
(4) Deed;
(5) Special statutory proceeding; or
(6) Condemnation.
(c) Types of roadways. The governing authority of Henry County has determined that there shall be the following classifications of unpaved roads in the county:
(1) Private roadways. A private roadway is a roadway that is created by an easement from a landowner to a certain individual or individuals and the use of said road can be controlled by the parties to the easement. In addition, private roadways are roadways that are not used for the general public purpose. It has been determined by the Henry County Code that there can be only one (1) building permit issued on a private easement. These private roadways will not be maintained by the county.
(2) Public roadways with rights-of-way of sixty (60) feet or greater. These are roadways that are used for the general public purpose and have rights-of-way of greater than sixty (60) feet. These roadways will be maintained by the county consistent with the maintenance policy developed by the Henry County Department of Transportation.
(3) Public roadways with deeded rights-of-way of fifty (50) feet or greater but less than sixty (60) feet. These are roadways that are used for the general public purpose and have deeded rights-of-way of fifty (50) feet or greater but less than sixty (60) feet. These roadway rights-of-way were deeded to the county at a time when a fifty-foot right-of-way was sufficient according to the ordinances in effect in Henry County at the time of acceptance. These roadways will be maintained by the county consistent with the maintenance policy developed by the Henry County Department of Transportation and in accordance with deeded rights-of-way.
(4) Public roadways with rights-of-way less than sixty (60) feet. These are roadways which are used for the general public purpose and have rights-of-way of less than sixty (60) feet. These roadways will be maintained by the county in accordance with the maintenance policy developed by the Henry County Department of Transportation, but will not be maintained to the same standard as roads categorized in paragraph (b) hereof, because of insufficient rights-of-way. These roads will be upgraded to paragraph (b) as rights-of-way of sixty (60) feet or greater and deeded to Henry County by adjoining landowners.
(d) Inventory of roads. The county shall maintain a road inventory showing all of the roads in Henry County and the classification of such roads as they exist in accordance with this statement of policy.
(Res. No. 85-14, 4-2-85)
Editor's note— Resolution No. 85-14, enacted April 2, 1985, did not specifically amend this Code; hence, codification of the substantive provisions of such resolution as a new § 3-5-5 is at the discretion of the editor.

